#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""rwa — the client half of the AutoRimmer file protocol (spec 1.4).

The mod under `Source/AutoRimmer/` owns a directory under the bench's save-data
folder: `commands/<id>.json` in, `results/<id>.json` out, `status.json` as a
~1Hz heartbeat, `journal/<sid>.ndjson` as the event stream. This script is the
other end of that: write a command, wait for its result, print it, exit
non-zero if the mod said no.

It carries ZERO game semantics. There is no verb table here and no
verb-specific formatting: any word that is not one of this script's own
subcommands is sent to the mod as an op, and the mod's own `unknown-op` error
lists what it actually knows. That is deliberate — a verb table client-side
would be a second contract to keep in sync with `VerbRegistry`, and it would
rot on the first spec that adds a verb. Same reasoning drives the pretty
printer: it is a generic JSON tree renderer, so a serializer that grows a field
shows the field the day it ships.

Three things this script knows that the mod cannot tell you:

  * WHERE the protocol root is. Two bench layouts exist (XDG_CONFIG_HOME
    isolation on this box, `-savedatafolder` on the Windows bench), so the root
    is derived from a candidate list, never hardcoded. `--root`/`RWA_ROOT` is
    the escape hatch; the bench itself is found through the workspace's
    existing `RIMWORLD_VAULT` convention.

  * WHETHER THE GAME IS ACTUALLY RUNNING, which is not the same question as
    "is the process up". RimWorld's tick loop is frame-bound and Hyprland
    delivers no frames to a hidden special workspace, so a parked bench without
    the launcher's render_unfocused fix runs at ~1-2 fps (~4 tps) and looks
    perfectly healthy in `ps` (FINDINGS.md section 4b). So liveness is graded
    into five states — down / menu / stalled / starved / ok — and each gets its
    own message. Only `down` tells you how to start the game.

  * HOW TO BE WATCHED. `rwa watch on` is one file write plus one hyprctl call:
    the launcher runs the game under mangohud with MANGOHUD_CONFIGFILE pointed
    at the bench's `config/mangohud-agent.conf`, and MangoHud re-reads that file
    while the game runs, so rewriting `fps_limit` re-caps a RUNNING game. The
    window is already parked on Hyprland's `special:rwagent` by a `silent`
    windowrule applied before launch; revealing it is a separate, independent
    step. No game-side code is involved in either half.

Everything else is protocol plumbing, plus transcripts: every command and
result is mirrored into a run directory keyed by the game's session id, so the
transcript, the journal file (`journal/<sid>.ndjson`) and every result's `sid`
field share one key. `rwa replay <run-dir>` re-sends a transcript's commands in
order, which is what makes "replayable" a testable claim rather than a hope. A
run longer than 999 calls continues in `<run>-s01`, `-s02`, … , each segment's
meta.json naming the one before and the one after.

House style: stdlib only, python3, no third-party deps — this has to run from a
bare shell on either bench.
"""

